# Line-ending policy: every text file is LF, in the repository AND in the
# working tree, on every platform.
#
# Why one rule rather than a list of extensions. Without this file, a Windows
# clone with `core.autocrlf=true` checks text files out as CRLF, and this
# repo's CI gates read files line by line:
#
#   check_file_size_ratchet.sh  reads *.conf      -> "unknown conf key ''"
#   check_command_surface.sh    reads *.conf      -> "unknown disposition ''"
#   check_command_surface.sh    reads lib.rs      -> "handler entry this gate
#                                                     cannot read: '...,\r'"
#
# The first version of this file pinned the extensions those gates were then
# known to read. Review found a third (`lib.rs`, via the gate's handler
# extractor) the list had missed. A list of "files someone reads line by line"
# is a list nobody can keep complete, and each miss surfaces as a gate failure
# on one platform only. One rule has nothing to miss: the Windows working tree
# becomes byte-identical to the tree Linux CI tests.
#
# The `lib.rs` case also shows why a passing local run proves nothing here.
# Git for Windows' gawk strips CR in text mode, so that gate PASSES on a CRLF
# checkout under Git Bash -- and FAILS under any Linux awk reading the same
# files, which is what a WSL shell on a Windows clone uses. Verified both ways.
#
# No file in this repository needs CRLF today: there is no .bat, .cmd, .sln,
# .vcxproj, .reg, .nsi or .wxs, and no blob in git is stored CRLF, so this rule
# changes how files are checked out and changes no content. If a Windows batch
# file is ever added, give it `text eol=crlf` here in the same change --
# cmd.exe misreads labels in LF batch files.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary: never normalise, never diff as text.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.ico           binary
*.icns          binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
*.exe           binary
*.dll           binary
*.wasm          binary
